<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awash River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Awash River is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> that flows from the central highlands towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> lowlands. In the fourteenth century Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> launched expansion southward across the Awash, incorporating regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> and several Muslim sultanates. The river marked an important frontier in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awash-river.jpg" alt="Awash River" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørg Sandkjær (Bjorg Sandkjaer) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awash_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
